<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vale of Pickering</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vale of Pickering is a low-lying plain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-yorkshire">North Yorkshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, between the North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire-wolds">Yorkshire Wolds</a>, drained by the River Derwent. At the end of the last Ice Age it held a glacial lake, and its former wetlands preserve major prehistoric sites, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> settlement of Star Carr and the long-occupied site at West Heslerton. It is a fertile farming region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vale-of-pickering.jpg" alt="Vale of Pickering" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gordon Hatton · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Potato_Nab_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2991199.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The vale is bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire-wolds">Yorkshire Wolds</a> escarpment to the south, the Corallian limestone foothills of the North York Moors to the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> coast to the east and the Howardian Hills to the west. Its level ground is covered by glacial drift over Jurassic sandstones and mudstones. The Derwent rises on the North York Moors near Scarborough, gathers the River Hertford from the east and the River Rye from the west, and leaves the vale through Malton and the Kirkham gorge to join the Ouse. As post-glacial Lake Pickering drained away it left rivers and marshes, all since drained by a network of canalised ditches.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The vale has been inhabited continuously since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a>. At Star Carr, on the lake's eastern shore, archaeologists have found evidence of a permanent house of 10,500 years ago and a platform of birch branches and brushwood, where waterlogging preserved organic remains and flint tools; the site was probably visited by four or five families who hunted, fished and gathered plants. Earth long barrows mark the coming of farming in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-roads">Roman roads</a> and villas show activity in Roman times. In 2011 excavations near Sherburn uncovered a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> settlement.</p><p>From the 11th to the 15th centuries castles rose at Helmsley and Pickering, with fortified manor houses and churches and the strip fields still visible at Middleton. Landowners of the 17th and 18th centuries built Wykeham Abbey, Nunnington Hall and Ebberston Hall. The older villages and towns stand close together along the spring line on the lake's old shoreline, in long narrow parishes running from the vale floor to the hills; the more scattered villages of the floor followed the drainage and enclosure of the carrs early in the 19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The vale is rural, and farming is its main source of work, with tourism and retailing in the market towns of Helmsley, Kirkbymoorside, Pickering and Malton and near the coast. Its main east-west roads follow the shoreline of the glacial lake, and a heritage railway runs from Pickering to Grosmont.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vale of Pickering” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Society was based on mixed farming on the chalk Wolds and in the <b>Vale of Pickering</b>, with large cemeteries of thousands of barrows.</blockquote><a href="/state/parisii-britain">Parisii (Britain)</a></li></ul></section>
